# Chemical Bonding: How Atoms Combine

> Atoms bond to shed energy and reach noble-gas outer shells. The
> bond type chosen — ionic, covalent, metallic, or merely
> intermolecular — fixes melting point, conductivity, and shape.

## 1. Why Bonds Form

[definition]
A **chemical bond** is a lasting attraction between atoms that
lowers the system's energy. The driving rule is the **octet rule**:
atoms gain, lose, or share electrons to complete an outer shell.
[/definition]

**Lewis dot symbols** track only valence electrons — Na has one
dot, Cl seven — making bond prediction simple dot bookkeeping.

@diagram tree id="bond-types-tree" layout="tree" aria="Main chemical bond types and their subcategories"
  "Chemical bonds" -> "Ionic (transfer)"
  "Chemical bonds" -> "Covalent (sharing)"
  "Chemical bonds" -> "Metallic (electron sea)"
  "Covalent (sharing)" -> "Nonpolar (F2)"
  "Covalent (sharing)" -> "Polar (HF)"
@/diagram

## 2. Ionic Bonds: Electron Transfer

Sodium (2, 8, 1) hands its lone valence electron to chlorine
(2, 8, 7):

@math label="eq:nacl"
\mathrm{Na \to Na^{+} + e^{-}}, \quad \mathrm{Cl + e^{-} \to Cl^{-}}
@/math

Opposite charges then pack into a giant **crystal lattice**; the
energy released in lattice formation drives the reaction.

| Property of ionic compounds | Reason |
| --- | --- |
| high melting points (NaCl 801 °C) | strong electrostatic lattice |
| conduct only when molten or dissolved | ions must be free to move |
| hard but brittle | shifted layers bring like charges face to face |
| often soluble in water | polar water molecules stabilize the ions |

## 3. Covalent Bonds: Electron Sharing

| Bond | Example | Shared pairs |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Single | H–H, Cl–Cl | 1 |
| Double | O=O (O₂) | 2 |
| Triple | N≡N (N₂) | 3 — strongest, shortest |

**Polarity** hinges on the electronegativity difference: in F₂
(ΔEN = 0) the pair sits dead centre — nonpolar; in HF (ΔEN = 1.9)
fluorine drags the pair its way — polar, with H δ+ and F δ−.

## 4. Metallic and Coordinate Bonds

**Metallic bonding**: metal kernels afloat in a delocalized
"electron sea" — hence conductivity, malleability, and shine.

[definition]
A **coordinate (dative) bond** shares a pair that one atom supplied
entirely. Once formed it behaves as any covalent bond. Classics:
the ammonium ion NH₄⁺, the hydronium ion H₃O⁺, and carbon monoxide.
[/definition]

## 5. Forces Between Molecules

| Force | Typical strength | Requirement | Example |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Hydrogen bond | ~10–40 kJ/mol | H bonded to N, O, or F | H₂O, NH₃, HF |
| Dipole–dipole | moderate | polar molecules | HCl |
| London (dispersion) | weakest; grows with size | temporary dipoles | F₂ gas vs I₂ solid |

[important]
**Water's hydrogen bonds explain its anomalies:** ice floats because
freezing locks molecules into an open, H-bonded cage less dense
than the liquid; the boiling point climbs to 100 °C while H₂S,
too weak to hydrogen-bond, boils at −60 °C.
[/important]

## 6. VSEPR: Predicting Shapes

[definition]
**VSEPR theory**: electron pairs around a central atom repel and
settle as far apart as possible. Lone pairs repel harder than bond
pairs, so they squeeze the angles.
[/definition]

| Shape | Angle | Bond pairs + lone pairs | Example |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Linear | 180° | 2 + 0 | BeCl₂, CO₂ |
| Trigonal planar | 120° | 3 + 0 | BF₃ |
| Tetrahedral | 109.5° | 4 + 0 | CH₄ |
| Trigonal pyramidal | 107° | 3 + 1 | NH₃ |
| Bent | 104.5° | 2 + 2 | H₂O |
| Octahedral | 90° | 6 + 0 | SF₆ |

## 7. Hybridization, Sigma and Pi

| Hybridization | Mixing | Geometry | Examples |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| sp | 1 s + 1 p | linear | BeCl₂, C₂H₂ |
| sp² | 1 s + 2 p | trigonal planar | BF₃, C₂H₄ |
| sp³ | 1 s + 3 p | tetrahedral | CH₄, NH₃, H₂O |

A **sigma (σ) bond** comes from head-on overlap — every single bond
and the first bond of any multiple bond. A **pi (π) bond** comes
from sideways overlap of parallel p orbitals — the second and third
bonds of double and triple bonds. Exposed π electrons make alkenes
the reactive corner of organic chemistry.

## 8. Bond Parameters

| Bond | Length (pm) | Energy (kJ/mol) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| H–H | 74 | 436 |
| C–H | 109 | 413 |
| O=O | 121 | 498 |
| C–C | 154 | 348 |
| C=C | 134 | 614 |
| C≡C | 120 | 839 |
| N≡N | 110 | 945 |

[note]
Multiplicity tightens and strengthens: compare C–C, C=C, C≡C —
shorter at each step, and harder to break at each step.
[/note]

## 9. Quick Revision

| Question | Answer |
| --- | --- |
| Metal + non-metal? | ionic transfer |
| Non-metal + non-metal? | covalent sharing |
| Which molecules hydrogen-bond? | H attached to N, O, or F |
| NH₃ shape and angle? | trigonal pyramidal, 107° |
| Why do metals conduct? | delocalized electron sea |
